WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.390
So, this video is part of my series on

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.550
the introduction to digital design using

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.750
VHDL, and I'm talking about finite state

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.870
machines and synchronous state machines.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.390
And in this video, I'm going to look at

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.870
using the algorithmic state machine

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.030
approach to design general finite state

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.430
machines

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.590
where we combine both the uh

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.070
Mealy approach and the Moore approach.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.510
So, we have outputs, uh next the next

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.070
state of the state machine, and the

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.830
outputs of the state machine are both

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.630
functions of the current state and the

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.150
current inputs. So, our next state in

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.790
our state machine, so this is an diagram

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.230
of a state machine.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.110
We have a as usual, we have our state

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.550
register, the output of that is our

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.870
current state.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.470
Uh we have some combinational logic

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.430
which the current state is fed into

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.070
together with external inputs that

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.190
determines the next state. So, that the

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.590
current the next state becomes the

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.310
current state on a clock edge. It's a

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.230
synchronous state machine. If we look at

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.110
outputs, we have two types of outputs.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.870
We have state outputs that are a

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.189
function just of the current state.

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:06.510
Then we also have immediate outputs

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:07.750
which are a function of the current

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.150
inputs and the current state.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.350
So, this this FSM provides both

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.990
immediate and state outputs.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.030
Um

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.990
so, the technique we use for designing

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.270
these is the algorithmic state machine

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.950
approach. There are many formal

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.790
techniques for describing finite state

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.630
machines such as uh set theory or

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.990
process algebra.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.910
Um in practice, uh this graphical

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.830
approach tangible graphic notation ASM

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:36.990
uh

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.550
really is uh much easier to use uh and

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:41.870
brings to the fore of the functional

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.470
specification of what we're trying to

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.350
achieve, but hides the underlying

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.150
mathematics. So, this is a more

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.630
practical approach than a purely

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.510
mathematical one.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:52.790
It's been developed specifically for the

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.270
use in digital systems um and it's a

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.710
structured approach. So, this is an

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.190
engineering structured approach to

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.950
developing a design

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.270
with an easily understood graphical

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.790
notation and with an underlying

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.469
implicit mathematical structure and

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:09.790
operational semantics.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.030
Um the other thing about using this

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.430
approach is this designer process is the

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.910
designs from different designers are

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.990
likely to converge

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.590
uh into a common solution

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.590
or solutions which have common features

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.350
that everybody can easily assimilate and

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.110
design. Um so, other designers can use.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.750
So, from an engineering point of view,

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.470
this approach this of uh

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.830
producing a design and communicating it

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.190
is very important in team teamwork uh

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.870
where the design is a common property of

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.390
the team.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.310
So,

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:43.990
the ASM we do uh we use what's called an

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.430
algorithmic state machine chart and we

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.030
start with the idea that we have a state

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.110
block. Now, I've drawn a white box a

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.470
shaded line around this is this the

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.350
scope of the state. Often, that's

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.670
actually left out of the diagram.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.790
The important thing here is I've got a

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.509
state box in the inside. So, we've got a

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:04.350
state block which can have several

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.470
things. In this one, it's just a state.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.990
We've got a single state box in the

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.630
inside.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.430
And it's got a state output list.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.910
Right? So, you write inside the box

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:17.630
which outputs what the outputs are.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.509
Um

00:03:18.519 --> 00:03:20.630
as with other models, you might just

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.870
write the list of outputs which are

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.070
asserted

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.870
or you might write in some Boolean

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.509
expressions for for uh

00:03:28.519 --> 00:03:30.830
those.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.550
But, these are state outputs, remember.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:33.990
Right? So, we've got a state box. With

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.350
that, we need two things. We need an

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.550
identifier, a state identifier, a label.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.590
And we might also assign a binary code

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.190
to the states. We might write that in

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.830
the diagram. When we're designing in

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.190
VHDL, we probably won't need to do that.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.270
We might only need the labels. Um but,

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.030
if you want, you can also write a state

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:52.950
identifier use as a binary code that

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.310
would be how it would be represented.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.830
So, this is a simple state.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.470
We've got and that the next state is

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:01.830
we've got a path coming out. We've got

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.470
an entry path coming into the state

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.030
block and an exit path going out.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.390
Uh we can only have one entry path.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.390
But, we could have multiple exit paths

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.870
going on to different subsequent states.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.470
So, the exit path to the new state the

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.150
next state is it just a function of the

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:17.990
current state.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.710
In this particular one because we've no

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.630
inputs and the outputs are just a

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.990
function of the current states. So, just

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.550
to say each state block has to have one

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.950
state box. All right, that's kind of

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.230
and the state outputs are listed.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.510
Transitions are given by the arcs.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.030
And the state label is an alphanumeric

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.470
label as before. I suggest if you're

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.550
designing for VHDL

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.990
and use a name that is valid in in in

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.270
the language in VHDL. And the state

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.110
identifier if we're writing them in the

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.190
in the ASM chart is a unique binary

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.670
code.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:53.990
So, with that we can simply we can

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.670
easily do counters.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.830
Um so, here is a set of states. In this

